  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe will use a grid optimization approach over a small number (k=2 to 4) of triangular pulses with full width base. Each pulse has three parameters: start position, peak position, and base width. The representation ensures disjoint pulses exactly covering the domain with integral=1. We will perform a grid search on these parameters to minimize the maximum correlation. This differs from the parent strategy's local optimization with diverse starting points by using a multi-dimensional grid search which explores the parameter space systematically.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\n- Representation:\n  Let M be the number of pulses (k=2-4)\n  Each pulse i has:\n    Start: a_i \u2208 [0,2]\n    Peak: c_i = a_i + b_i (midpoint)\n    Base: b_i \u2208 [min_base, max_base]\n\n  Pulse function: \n    f_i(x) = 0 for x \u2260 [a_i, a_i+b_i]\n    f_i(x) = 2\u00b7(x-a_i)/b_i for x \u2208 [a_i, a_i+b_i/2]\n    f_i(x) = 2\u00b7(1-(x-a_i)/b_i) for x \u2208 [a_i+b_i/2, a_i+b_i]\n\n  Total function: h(x) = \u03a3 f_i(x) for i=1..M\n\n  Constraints:\n    \u222b h(x) dx over [0,2] = 1 (automatically satisfied with M pulses)\n    0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 (satisfied by disjoint pulses)\n    Integral constraint: sum(h) \u2248 140.0 (for discrete case)\n\n  Objective:\n    C_5 = max_k [\u222b h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx] (discrete correlation max)\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\na. Initialization:\n   - Set n_points = 280\n   - dx = 2/n_points\n   - base_widths = [0.5, 0.4, 0.3] for different M\n\nb. Pulse Generation:\n   - For given M and parameters (a_i, c_i, b_i), create discrete samples:\n     h_vals = np.zeros(n_points)\n     For each pulse i:\n        Compute x_i = sorted indices\n        Use linear interpolation between in-points for triangular shape\n        Handle boundaries properly\n\nc. Correlation Calculation:\n   - Compute full mode correlation\n   - c5 = dx * max(corr)\n\nd. Optimization Flow:\n   - For M in range(2,5):\n        Define parameter bounds:\n          a_i \u2208 [0, 2 - max_base]\n          c_i \u2208 [base/2, 2 - base/2]\n          b_i = fixed/variable base from base_widths\n        Generate grid = gridsearch(grid_count=40)\n        Use bounds, no explicit constraints\n        For each grid point (set of (a_i, c_i, b_i) for i=1..M):\n            Build h\n            Clamp values to [0,1]\n            Compute correlation\n            Track minimum c5\n        Choose best configuration for each M\n   - Return best overall candidate\n\n   Helper functions needed:\n        np.clip() for clamping\n        np.interp() for linear interpolation\n        np.correlate() for correlation\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\n- Fixed grid search with 40 points per dimension\n- Base width varies for different M values: \n  M=2: bases around 0.5\n  M=3: bases around 0.4\n  M=4: bases around 0.3\n- Optimization points: (0.8, 0.99) in time\n- Stop after M=4 if time exceeds\n\n   For each M, we use:\n      Position start bounds: (0.0, 2.0 - base)\n      For M=2: two parameters (pulse starts)\n      For M=3: three parameters\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n\n  - Dominant cost: Discrete correlation O(n^2)\n  - For n=280, each correlation ~583 points, 280*583 ~163,240 operations\n  - With 40 samples per dimension, time complexity: \n      O(M^2 * grid_count^M * ops)\n  - Coefficients:\n      M=2: (grid_count^2) * 10 operations\n      M=3: (grid_count^3) * 10\n      M=4: (grid_count^4) * 10\n  - Estimate:\n      M=2: 40^2=1600 calls, each 10 ops => 16000 operations (total)\n      M=3: 40^3=64000 calls => too expensive\n\n  - Adjustment: Use only up to grid_count=4 for any dimension\n  - Termination: After M=2 optimization if time exceeds 0.4s\n  - Time estimation: ~0.45s per configuration\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\n- Validate:\n  - Check sum(h) \u2248 140.0\n  - Check h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x\n  - Compute correlation exactly\n  - Verify that pulses are disjoint\n\n- Return format:\n  (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\n  Feasibility measures:\n    - Clip any out-of-bound values\n    - Adjust pulse heights if overlap detected\n\nThe key innovation is applying systematic grid search to minimize overlap over explicit pulse parameters. This avoids local optima found via general-purpose local optimization. Through this structured parameter space exploration we seek better overlap bounds while maintaining pulse disjointness required for validity.",
